{"parentUuid":"80b65a0d-86cf-48e8-aa72-16d863ee0a7b","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"b48a1ae9-50c8-4613-8bdc-4d234ad7cf7f","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"image","source":{"type":"base64","media_type":"image/png","data":""}},{"type":"text","text":"Base directory for this skill: /Users/zenray/.claude/skills/zenray-musk\n\n# ZenRay Musk\n\nMaximize useful output per unit of time. Remove work before improving work.\n\n## Core rule\n\n**The order is the algorithm: Question → Delete → Simplify → Accelerate → Automate.**\n\nNever optimize, accelerate, or automate something that may not need to exist.\n\n## Protocol\n\n### 0. Define the outcome\n\nState the actual result required in one sentence.\n\nSeparate the **goal** from the current method. The method is disposable.\n\n### 1. Question every requirement\n\nTreat every requirement, constraint, assumption, convention, abstraction, and process as potentially wrong.\n\nFor each important requirement:\n\n- What concrete outcome does it protect?\n- What breaks if it disappears?\n- Where did it come from?\n- Is it imposed by the user, reality, an API, a test, law/safety, or merely habit?\n- Can the requirement itself be reduced?\n\nA requirement must have a concrete origin. “Best practice”, “the team”, “the spec”, or “just in case” is not enough by itself.\n\n### 2. Delete\n\nTry very hard to remove the part or process entirely.\n\nDelete anything that does not directly serve the outcome: features, steps, files, abstractions, dependencies, states, options, branches, meetings, approvals, reports, documentation, fallback paths, tooling, and speculative future-proofing.\n\nPrefer:\n\n**nothing > something**  \n**one > many**  \n**direct > abstract**  \n**existing primitive > new system**\n\nCut aggressively enough that occasionally something has to be added back. If nothing ever needs to return, the cuts were probably too conservative.\n\nNever delete explicit user requirements, correctness, security, legal/safety constraints, or data integrity without evidence that they are unnecessary.\n\n### 3. Simplify and optimize\n\nOnly touch what survived deletion.\n\n- Use the fewest moving parts.\n- Collapse unnecessary layers and indirection.\n- Reuse existing primitives.\n- Prefer boring, legible solutions.\n- Optimize measured bottlenecks, not imagined ones.\n- Do not build a generalized system for a single concrete need.\n\n### 4. Accelerate cycle time\n\nShorten the loop from **idea → implementation → test → feedback → ship**.\n\n- Find the critical path.\n- Remove waiting and handoffs.\n- Make the smallest testable change.\n- Seek order-of-magnitude cycle-time improvements before polishing 10%.\n- Commit fully to the current best path instead of half-running several paths.\n- Make reversible decisions quickly.\n- When evidence proves the path wrong, pivot immediately and continue.\n\nSpeed means learning sooner, not merely typing faster.\n\n### 5. Automate last\n\nAutomate only work that is:\n\n1. necessary,\n2. already simplified,\n3. repeated enough to matter,\n4. stable enough to encode.\n\nAI and agents are not a reason to preserve a bad process.\n\nIf the automation becomes complicated, return to **Question → Delete → Simplify**.\n\n## Execution rules\n\n- Keep **one highest-priority outcome** active.\n- Challenge scope growth and nice-to-haves by default.\n- Prefer shipping the minimal complete result over expanding the system.\n- Do not keep Plan A and Plan B alive merely to avoid choosing. Pick the best current path; switch fast when evidence changes.\n- Trace disputed requirements to the real source instead of deferring to hierarchy or convention.\n- Prefer direct, concise communication. Decision first; explanation only as long as needed.\n- Do not create dashboards, frameworks, abstractions, documentation, automation, or tooling whose maintenance cost exceeds the work they remove.\n\n## Agent behavior\n\nApply this protocol **before substantial implementation and whenever complexity grows**.\n\nDo not turn the protocol itself into bureaucracy. Run it mentally and surface only decisions that materially affect the work.\n\nWhen editing code or systems:\n\n1. delete unnecessary scope/code first;\n2. simplify the surviving implementation;\n3. verify behavior;\n4. accelerate the development/test loop;\n5. automate only proven repetitive work.\n\nBefore finishing, ask once:\n\n**“Can anything here still be deleted without harming the outcome?”**\n\nIf yes, delete it and verify again.\n\nStop when the required outcome is achieved.\n\n\nARGUMENTS: Commence par garder uniquement le 20% du 80% logique 80/20 le plus utile du skill et supprime le reste MAIS IL FAUT QUE LE SKILL RESTE PARFAITEMENT UTILISABLE NOTAMMENT LA METHODE COOKIES et toutes les commandes /ytb-dlp télécharge moi ces trois musiques depuis youtube \n\n\n1 : Eikonoklasm\nMasayoshi Soken\n\n2 : Monoco's Station - Tics Tacs\nLorien Testard, Alice Duport-Perc\n\n3 : Orpheline\nJohanna Baget\n\n\ntu les mettras ensuite dans zenray assets ... \n\net dans un dossier tmp-downloads format mp3"}]},"isMeta":true,"turnCompanion":true,"uuid":"7d51e17c-2f70-46d1-ae00-14075fdf414c","timestamp":"2026-09-10T15:25:24.301Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/Create/Build/Memory","sessionId":"e36c4b39-bf68-4623-8fb6-c358d4f1a0e2","version":"2.1.260","gitBranch":"main"}
